#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "32f7b0e6-5a68-52ab-9d86-ea71387cfb77" :question/math-academy-id "e-10975" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/cdd0cbcb7e4fcb26862a5ab33958edac.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents the whole plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n- Diagram III is a parabola that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/id #uuid "554fc7b1-62ca-5189-bda1-712f518ad617" :question/math-academy-id "e-10976" :question/problem "Consider the subsets $\\; A=\\{\\langle x, y, z \\rangle \\in \\mathbb{R}^3: x^2=y \\}\\;$ and $\\; B= \\{\\langle 3-t, -3+t, -6+2t \\rangle: t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\\;$ Which of them is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $A$, but the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 - t \\\\ -3 + t \\\\ -6 + 2t\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich gives us\n${3 - t = 0; - 3 + t = 0; - 6 + 2t = 0$.\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3$, then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B$. Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192333 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/id #uuid "6e80df98-c67c-5f27-a44b-d0bc8f681ac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10979" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^3$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/31239f102a0bab45ca4ad0b3cb2e9ccb.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n- Diagram II represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is also a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b7890e3-ca7f-5c9e-8ca5-e974b3ffd737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191623} {:answer/id #uuid "b60354b8-dd3d-5a04-9c30-3cfc4e5b66cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191624} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fd18e8-322b-5bde-ada0-802ae5e9b688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191625} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4595cf-e6b7-50bf-9502-950360fd3062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191626} {:answer/id #uuid "75ebdeb3-14df-5be5-84e0-d1fc55f64ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b7890e3-ca7f-5c9e-8ca5-e974b3ffd737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191623} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3755f6a-5c48-5d4d-87e3-d045a18cf1fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191622}] :question/id #uuid "028630ef-e1bc-5d42-ac61-af7d94939f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34318" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/q-34318.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $R^{2}$?"}]}
